WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.475
Have you ever wondered what happens when
you click a link?

00:00:03.595 --> 00:00:05.665
You click a link or type an address,

00:00:05.665 --> 00:00:07.678
and just like that, a page appears.

00:00:07.798 --> 00:00:10.352
First, the name. Your browser
can't use it,

00:00:10.352 --> 00:00:12.845
because the internet only speaks numbers.

00:00:12.965 --> 00:00:14.825
So it asks a resolver:

00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:18.208
what's the number behind layrus dot com?

00:00:18.328 --> 00:00:19.953
Then it opens a connection,

00:00:19.953 --> 00:00:22.000
and proves who's on the other end.

00:00:22.044 --> 00:00:24.328
The address starts with https,

00:00:24.328 --> 00:00:28.136
so the browser knows this page has to
be encrypted.

00:00:28.256 --> 00:00:30.872
It opens a connection and says hello:

00:00:30.872 --> 00:00:34.477
the TLS versions it speaks, the ciphers
it supports,

00:00:34.477 --> 00:00:35.891
and a random number.

00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:39.550
The server replies with the version and
cipher it chose,

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:42.253
its own random number, and its
certificate.

00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:46.171
That certificate is the server's ID: who
it claims to be,

00:00:46.171 --> 00:00:48.545
who issued it, and when it expires.

00:00:48.665 --> 00:00:49.599
First check:

00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:54.269
does the name on the certificate match the
address you typed?

00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:54.989
Second:

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.113
does the issuer chain back to a root the
browser already

00:00:59.113 --> 00:00:59.563
trusts?

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:03.859
Third: are the dates valid, and has the
certificate been revoked?

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:06.769
Then the server proves it holds the
private key,

00:01:06.769 --> 00:01:10.449
and both sides derive the same session key
without ever sending

00:01:10.449 --> 00:01:10.627
it.

00:01:10.747 --> 00:01:13.610
From that moment, everything is encrypted.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:15.587
The padlock is not a sticker;

00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:18.313
it is what four passed checks look like.

00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:19.657
If any check fails,

00:01:19.657 --> 00:01:23.329
the browser stops and shows a warning
instead of the page:

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:27.323
an expired certificate, the wrong name, or
an untrusted issuer.

00:01:27.443 --> 00:01:30.262
And if a site offers no encryption at all,

00:01:30.262 --> 00:01:32.411
the browser marks it not secure,

00:01:32.411 --> 00:01:35.700
and HSTS can refuse to load it over
http entirely.

00:01:36.099 --> 00:01:38.429
Only then does the request go out.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:40.472
The server answers with the page.

00:01:40.592 --> 00:01:42.364
And the browser paints it.

00:01:42.484 --> 00:01:45.290
All of it, in under a second.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.067
The internet isn't a place.

00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:49.200
It's an agreement about how to
pass a message.
